# config/private-material-allow - identities that are legitimately public for
# this home. Copy this into a local, gitignored config/private-material-allow
# and edit. One token per line; blank lines and "#" comments are ignored.
#
# bin/fm-private-material-check.sh derives its markers at runtime from this
# home's private dirs (project clone names, registered project and secondmate
# ids, git remote owners, the local account name). That derivation is
# deliberately blunt, so it also picks up identities that are already public and
# must stay in the tracked surface - most commonly the upstream owner this repo
# was forked from, whose name appears in install URLs, badges, and clone
# instructions.
#
# List those here. The check refuses rather than guessing: silently allowing an
# owner it could not classify would be exactly the failure mode that publishes
# private material, so an unclassified owner fails loudly and you decide.
#
# Example - the upstream project this fleet forked from, and a vendor whose
# name is in a tracked install URL:
# upstream-owner
# some-vendor

# The sibling file config/private-material-markers uses the same one-token-per-
# line format for the opposite job: markers nothing can derive from local state,
# such as a device serial, a customer, or a person's name.
